Z||000000||CozmoPooky||11-28-2000||08:12 AM||||I have an '85 2M6 4spd. Targa that the prev. owner put a replacement (salvage yard) engine in. The new engine has an engraved intake plenum so I assume it is out of an '87-'88. The original '85 ECM is still in the car. I have heard there were differences in power ratings between the early and late model V-6's. Is this true, and if so is it because of the ECM or the engine? (BTW, the car runs extremely strong).<BR>  <P>------------------<BR>Bob Cardone (CozmoPooky)<BR>'87 Coupe<BR>'85 2M6 Targa<p>[This message has been edited by CozmoPooky (edited 11-28-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Monkeyman||11-28-2000||07:51 PM||||I've got the same setup.  '85 GT with an '87 V6.  The motor's tired so I don't know if there's any loss in power, but I think the loss of power in the later motors was because of some smog related add ons.  There's only about a 5 hp difference so you shouldn't really notice it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||rockcrawl||11-28-2000||08:31 PM||||There is no actual power difference between any of the Fiero 2.8s. The 87-88s were tested at a different RPM to meet changing emisions requirements. The 5hp difference is due to being tested at a different RPM. They all have the same emisions equipment (ecxept maybe the ECM, but 86-88 use the same ECM).<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fiero_Freak||11-28-2000||09:56 PM||||   if you'd be going with another 2.8 later you could always get an aftermarket chip.||||reg||1
